<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Badr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/624">624</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Day of the Criterion, Yawm al-Furqan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Badr was fought on 13 March 624 (17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>, 2 AH) near Badr, in present-day Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Muhammad, with an army of about 313 men, defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> force of around 1300 led by Amr ibn Hisham, known to Muslims as Abu Jahl. It began when Muhammad set out to intercept a Meccan caravan under Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, which escaped by another route. About 70 Meccans were killed, including Amr ibn Hisham and Umayya ibn Khalaf, and about 70 were taken prisoner. Muslim tradition calls it a victory owed to divine intervention; other sources credit Muhammad's strategy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 622 the people of Medina chose Muhammad as leader of the community, and his followers decided to raid the caravans of the Meccans. In early 624 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> caravan under Abu Sufyan ibn Harb was returning from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, carrying merchandise worth 50,000 dinars and guarded by 70 men.</p><p>Muhammad decided to intercept it, to recover wealth the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> had confiscated from Muslims after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a> and to impress the Meccans as a deterrent against an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Abu Sufyan sent the messenger Damdam ibn Amr al-Ghifari to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> for help, and Damdam is said to have cut off the nose and ears of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> on arrival at the Ka'bah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Muhammad gathered an army of 313 to 317 men, the generally accepted number being 313: 82 Muhajirun, 61 men of the Aws and 170 of the Khazraj. All the clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> except the Banu Adi assembled an army, and at al-Juhfah, on learning that the caravan was safe, the Banu Zuhrah, around 300 men, turned back. At a council of war Abu Bakr, Umar, Miqdad ibn Aswad and then Sa'd ibn Mu'adh for the Ansar pledged their support.</p><p>On the advice of al-Hubab ibn al-Mundhir the Muslims occupied the well closest to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> and destroyed the others. The battle began with duels, in which Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib killed al-Aswad ibn Abd al-Asad and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, Shayba ibn Rabi'a and al-Walid ibn Utba. Muhammad threw a handful of pebbles and ordered a counter-attack, and the Meccan lines broke.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Some 70 prisoners were taken. Abu Bakr advised ransom and Umar execution, and Muhammad accepted ransom. Scholars estimate Meccan dead at around 70, but only the more prominent names are known: Amr ibn Hisham, Umayya ibn Khalaf, Utba ibn Rabi'a, Shayba ibn Rabi'a and al-Walid ibn Utba among them. The victory turned Muhammad from a Meccan outcast into leader of a community and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>.</p><p>Marshall Hodgson writes that other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> came to regard the Muslims as challengers and potential inheritors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> prestige. Abu Sufyan, safe with the caravan, became chief of the Quraysh by default after the death of Amr ibn Hisham and many nobles, and helped negotiate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>'s surrender six years later. All knowledge of the battle comes from traditional Islamic accounts recorded later, such as those of the 8th century, and some historians think the historicity debatable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Badr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
